Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 10:08:40 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 030/154] test: hold the Keyhole to its promise instead of
 trusting the source
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The Keyhole's whole claim is that a pinned page never navigates away when you
click an off-site link. That behaviour shipped long ago and had no test
over it anywhere - the decision lived inline in ShouldAutoPeek, tangled with a
WebContents, a Browser and a call across the dylib boundary, so there was
nothing a unit test could reach.

The five predicates move into atrium_peek_decision.h as bools in, bool out.
Same posture as atrium_routing_navigation_decision.h, which did this for the
routing throttle for the same reason. ShouldAutoPeek keeps the job of
resolving those bools from live objects, and keeps its short-circuits:
IsPeekSourceTab crosses into Swift and should not be called speculatively.

Seven tests, and one of them is the point - every predicate is individually
necessary, asserted by flipping each field of an otherwise-peeking input and
requiring the peek to disappear. A decision table that had quietly become an
OR of two fields would satisfy every other case in the file and still be
wrong.

Checked they can fail rather than assuming it: inverting the same-site term
turns three of the seven red, including the same-site case by name. Restored
and green again.

No behaviour change. Same-site still navigates in place on purpose, so a
pinned Gmail or Notion stays usable as an app, and the test now says so where
the reasoning cannot drift away from the code.

Signed-off-by: János Mikó <693558+janosmiko@users.noreply.github.com>
